### What happens

A Solidity enum links its values to the enum with a `contains` edge, the same relation used for real declared members (struct fields, state variables). Every other language that has enums emits a `case_of` edge per value — Java (#1719), C#, Swift, Rust, and VB.NET (#4054, the previous outlier that was fixed). Solidity is now the last extractor still using `contains` for enum values.

### Minimal reproduction

```solidity
// Status.sol
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;
contract Order {
    enum Status { Pending, Shipped, Delivered }
    struct Point { uint x; uint y; }
}
```

```python
from graphify.extract import extract
res = extract(["Status.sol"])
for e in res["edges"]:
    if e["relation"] in ("case_of", "contains"):
        print(e["relation"], e["source"], "->", e["target"])
```

### Expected

`Status -> Pending/Shipped/Delivered` as `case_of` (struct field `Point.x` stays `contains`).

### Actual

`Status -> Pending/Shipped/Delivered` as `contains`, indistinguishable from a struct field.

### Why it matters

This is not cosmetic. `case_of` targets are deliberately excluded from constructor binding (`_member_nids` in `extract.py`), so an enum value whose name collides with a type can currently be mistaken for a constructable member. It also breaks the cross-language invariant that downstream consumers rely on to tell enum cases apart from declared fields.

