A Java anonymous class (`new Runnable() { … }`) contributes nothing to the graph: its methods are not nodes, and every call made inside them is dropped. Anonymous listeners/callbacks are routine in Java, so a large slice of the call graph silently disappears. Kotlin's equivalent (`object : Foo { … }`, #2347) is already handled — Java is the gap.

### Minimal reproduction
```java
