## Summary

An ObjC class whose `@interface` and `@implementation` live in **different directories** (e.g. `include/Widget.h` + `src/Widget.m`) loses all resolved member-call edges into it. The same corpus resolves fine when the pair shares a directory.

## Root cause

`_merge_decl_def_classes` folds a decl/def pair into a single class node only when both files share the **same directory** — the node id embeds the directory path (`#1556`). A header/impl directory split therefore mints two same-labelled `Widget` nodes that never merge.

`_resolve_objc_member_calls` then sees two type-def candidates for the receiver and bails on its single-definition god-node guard:

```python
if len(type_defs) != 1:  # ambiguous -> bail (god-node guard)
    continue
```

So `[w render]` emits **no** `calls` edge, even though exactly one of the two nodes is the real `@implementation`.

## Reproduce

```
include/Widget.h:  @interface Widget : NSObject
                   - (void)render;
                   @end
src/Widget.m:      #import "Widget.h"
                   @implementation Widget
                   - (void)render {}
                   @end
src/Caller.m:      #import "Widget.h"
                   @implementation Caller
                   - (void)go { Widget *w = [Widget new]; [w render]; }
                   @end
```

`extract --no-cluster` on this produces two `Widget` nodes, two `-render` nodes (decl + def), and **no** `-go -> -render` edge.

## Expected

`-go` resolves to the sole `-render` **definition** (the `.m` node). The guard should still fire for the genuinely-ambiguous case: two *unrelated* same-named classes that each define the selector.

## Fix

Union the candidates' members and bind the selector to the one candidate that **defines** it (an `@implementation` in `.m`/`.mm`, or a merged node carrying `definition_file`). A header-only node is a bare declaration and does not count. Two definitions of the same selector stay ambiguous — the guard holds for the case it was meant for.

Header/impl directory splits are a common layout, so this affects ordinary code, not an edge case.

Found while auditing lesser-used language extractors for unreported bugs; no existing issue or PR covers this.

The Zig extractor's call resolver computes `is_member_call = "." in fn_text` for every call site, but never actually uses it: `tgt_nid = next((n["id"] for n in nodes if n["label"] in (f"{callee}()", f".{callee}()")), None)` accepts either the free-function label or the method label for every call regardless of how it was written. A bare call (`helper()`) can therefore resolve onto an unrelated struct's method of the same name when no free function by that name exists in the file, and a member call (`obj.helper()`) can just as easily resolve onto an unrelated free function when no method by that name exists on the receiver's type — whichever declaration happens to come first in the file's own node list wins, regardless of which one the call shape actually names.

Reproduced directly:
```zig
const Box = struct {
    pub fn helper(self: Box) i32 { return 1; }
};
fn helper() i32 { return 2; }
fn caller() i32 { return helper(); }
```
Before the fix, `caller()`'s bare call to `helper()` resolves to `Box.helper` (the method) instead of the free function - confirmed via the extractor's own output. The symmetric case (a member call landing on an unrelated free function) reproduces the same way with the call site swapped.

Fix: the already-computed `is_member_call` flag now picks which single label the lookup accepts - `.{callee}()` for a dotted call, `{callee}()` for a bare one - so a call can only ever bind the kind of declaration its own syntax names.

Added two regression tests, one per direction, each with a free function and an unrelated struct method sharing one name so the collision is unambiguous. Verified pre-fix behavior via `git show HEAD:<path>` + a temporary file swap (never `git stash`), confirmed byte-identical restoration afterward. Full suite: identical failure set to a freshly checked-out, unmodified `upstream/v8` (confirmed by running the exact same 44 test node IDs against a separate clean worktree - all 44 fail there too, none related to this change). `tools.skillgen --check` clean.

## Summary

Fixes #3667.

A cross-file resolver can fail without aborting extraction, but before this change that degradation existed only as a transient log warning. The persisted graph could therefore look healthy while an entire relationship layer was missing.

This change makes resolver degradation durable and self-describing:

- records failures from both registry-backed and hand-wired resolution passes;
- persists sanitized failure records as top-level `degraded_passes` in `graph.json`;
- preserves a failure across unrelated incremental updates;
- clears a stale failure only after that same resolver runs successfully;
- surfaces the same state under **Graph Health** in `GRAPH_REPORT.md` and through `graphify diagnose`;
- redacts secret-shaped text before an exception message can be persisted;
- keeps successful-pass status run-only, so healthy graphs do not gain persistent noise.

The existing fail-soft behavior is unchanged: one resolver failure still does not block later passes or prevent a usable partial graph from being written. The difference is that downstream agents can now tell the graph is degraded.

