First of all, thank you for the very quick response on #4874! We upgraded to 0.9.100 the day it came out, and it solved a lot for us. In our private mixed Android project, `graphify affected` on a widely used Java class went from 0 of 36 Kotlin importers to 36 of 36, and imports and inheritance now resolve in both directions.

While validating the upgrade, we noticed a few remaining inconsistencies in `calls` edges from Kotlin. They are narrower than the original report, so we are opening a new issue rather than reopening #4874.

### What we expected

Each call below should produce a `calls` edge to the method that is actually called, or no edge when the target is outside the repository:

1. A Kotlin call to an overloaded Java method links to that method, as the equivalent Java call does.
2. A Kotlin call inside an `init` block links to the called method.
3. A call to a method inherited from an external class does not link to an unrelated class that happens to have a method with the same name.

### What actually happened

1. **Overloaded Java method called from Kotlin:** no edge is created. The non-overloaded method in the same class resolves from the same Kotlin file, and Java callers resolve in both cases.
2. **Call inside a Kotlin `init` block:** no edge is created. The class has no node for the `init` block, so the call has no source to attach to. This may be related to #2565, which covered property initializers.
3. **Inherited external method:** `KotlinScreen.title()` calls `getString` inherited from `android.app.Activity`, but gets an `INFERRED` `calls` edge to the unrelated `Prefs.getString()`. This one may have a different cause from the other two, so please feel free to split it out.

`graphify affected` depends on these edges, so case 1 and case 2 silently omit callers, and case 3 lists callers that are not real.

### Steps to reproduce

Tested on a clean folder with 0.9.100:

```shell
# src/com/example/util/Logger.java
package com.example.util;
public class Logger {
    public static int single(String tag) { return 0; }
    public static int overloaded(String tag) { return 0; }
    public static int overloaded(String tag, String msg) { return 0; }
}

# src/com/example/util/Prefs.java
package com.example.util;
public class Prefs {
    public String getString(String key) { return key; }
}

# src/com/example/app/JavaCaller.java
package com.example.app;
import com.example.util.Logger;
public class JavaCaller {
    public void callSingle() { Logger.single("a"); }
    public void callOverloaded() { Logger.overloaded("a"); }
}

# src/com/example/app/KotlinCaller.kt
package com.example.app
import com.example.util.Logger
class KotlinCaller {
    fun callSingle() { Logger.single("a") }
    fun callOverloaded() { Logger.overloaded("a") }
}

# src/com/example/app/KotlinInit.kt
package com.example.app
import com.example.util.Logger
class KotlinInit {
    init { Logger.single("a") }
}

# src/com/example/app/KotlinScreen.kt
package com.example.app
import android.app.Activity
class KotlinScreen : Activity() {
    fun title(): String = getString(1)
}

graphify update .
# then list the edges with relation "calls" in graphify-out/graph.json
```

### Graph output

All `calls` edges in `graph.json`:

```text
JavaCaller.java   .callOverloaded() -> Logger.java .overloaded() [EXTRACTED]
JavaCaller.java   .callSingle()     -> Logger.java .single()     [EXTRACTED]
KotlinCaller.kt   .callSingle()     -> Logger.java .single()     [EXTRACTED]
KotlinScreen.kt   .title()          -> Prefs.java  .getString()  [INFERRED]   (unrelated class)

Missing:
KotlinCaller.kt   .callOverloaded() -> Logger.java .overloaded()
KotlinInit.kt     (init block)      -> Logger.java .single()
```

### Impact on a real project

In our project, 38 Kotlin files call a Java logging class whose main methods are overloaded, and the graph links only 1 of them. The one that links is a call to a non-overloaded method. We also see 121 `INFERRED` Kotlin calls to one Java class's `getString()`, which look like case 3, although we have not checked each one.

For now, we keep confirming callers with `git grep`, which works fine as a workaround.

